t":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":1,"created_at":"2025-10-13T13:07:09Z","updated_at":"2025-10-13T13:15:46Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproducible in 4.6.dev.custom_build [4219ce91f], 4.5.stable\n\n### System information\n\nWindows 11 - Godot v4.6.dev.custom_build [4219ce91f] - D3D12 (Forward+) - Dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4080 Laptop GPU\n\n### Issue description\n\nSetting Vector Image Editor explicitly still uses the OS defined SVG app (Which is set to Microsoft Edge in Windows 11 in this case).\n\nThe vector graphics in question that I want to open into an external editor is a SVG file imported as `DPITexture`. I tested opening Raster images (such as a WEBP image as `Texture2D`) and it opened using the defined editor (**PixiEditor** in this case, whereas it is set to open using the **Photos** app in Windows File Explorer).\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n- Import a SVG file to the project or use the Godot Icon.\n- Set it to `DPITexture` as import preset.\n- Set an External Editor for Vector images in Editor Settings at `filesystem/external_programs/vector_image_editor`\n<img width=\"1204\" height=\"356\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/bae1e2f2-2337-4e2e-944b-23f6cd5cbb0f\" />\n\n- Right click the SVG file and select Open in External Program or press `Ctrl + Alt + E` in the filesystem.\n\n<img width=\"513\" height=\"128\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/00063a98-107a-47fc-a11e-6927ee9b3ced\" />\n\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nA new project.","closed_by":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.git